== General election ==
[[Cost of living]], the [[economy]], [[War|foreign wars]], and the candidates' experience were the central issues for voters.<ref name="Mazzei1">{{Cite news |last1= Mazzei |first1= Patricia |last2= Igielnik |first2= Ruth |date= October 28, 2026 |title= The Senate Race in Heavily Republican South Carolina is a Tossup, Poll Finds |url= https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/28/us/south-carolina-florida-minnesota-poll.html |access-date= October 29, 2026 |work= The New York Times |language= en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

The seat was initially considered safe for Republicans as South Carolina is normally considered a strongly [[red state]] at the state and federal levels, but after Graham said during a primary runoff debate that "[[national security]] is not my thing,” some voters began to see her as inexperienced and unqualified, inflaming a divide between establishment and conservative Republicans<ref>{{Cite news |date=November 3, 2026 |title=Congressman Ralph Norman reacts to Annie Andrews beating Darline Graham for SC Senate seat |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GLkuf5sCRmk |access-date=November 6, 2026 |work=Queen City News}}</ref> who objected to Trump's involvement in the race,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Gallagher |first=Dianne |date=November 4, 2026 |title=Trump's personal push of Darline Graham in South Carolina comes back to bite his party |url= https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/03/politics/trump-darline-graham-loss |access-date=November 8, 2026 |work= CNN |language=en}}</ref> and the race became a contested tossup.<ref name= "Mazzei1" /> A ''[[The New York Times]]/[[Siena University (New York)|Siena]]'' poll found that "51 percent of the voters in the polls, including 18 percent of Republicans" said Graham "did not have the right experience to be an effective senator". In contrast, "54 percent of voters, including 22 percent of Republicans, said that Dr. Andrews did."<ref name="Mazzei1" />

